  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0152\" contains 9 tables.\nThe \"abilities\" table is used to store information about abilities, including their id, name, title, entity id and entity type, whether they are only owned, options, scope, and timestamps for creation and update.\nThe \"users\" table stores user information, including their id, name, email, password, and timestamps for creation and update.\nThe \"model_has_abilities\" table is a junction table that links abilities and models, including the ability id, model type, model id, and scope.\nThe \"roles\" table stores role information, including their id, name, title, level, scope, and timestamps for creation and update.\nThe \"tenants\" table stores tenant information, including their id and name, and timestamps for creation and update.\nThe \"assigned_roles\" table is used to assign roles to entities, including the role id, entity id, entity type, restricted to id and type, and scope.\nThe \"permissions\" table stores permission information, including the ability id, entity id and entity type, whether they are forbidden, and scope.\nThe \"activity_logs\" table logs activity, including the loggable type, loggable id, action, user id, and timestamp for creation.\nFinally, the \"model_has_roles\" table is a junction table that links roles and models, including the role id, model type, model id, and scope.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a framework-agnostic role and ability management system utilizing Laravel Eloquent models. This solution allows dynamic assignment of roles and granular abilities to users, supporting both direct and role-based permissions. It is designed to efficiently manage and cache permissions, integrate with Laravel’s authorization system, and support multi-tenancy, making it adaptable to various application contexts. The system employs a database-driven approach to role and ability management, catering to applications that require fine-grained access control.\n\nObjectives\n\n1. Create a versatile role and ability management system compatible with any application using Eloquent models.\n2. Enable dynamic assignment and management of user roles and abilities.\n3. Ensure seamless integration with Laravel’s existing authorization mechanisms.\n4. Support multi-tenancy to handle permissions across different application contexts.\n5. Optimize performance through efficient caching of permissions.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Implement and maintain the system, ensuring it integrates well with existing applications.\n- Administrators: Manage user roles and abilities, ensuring correct permissions are assigned.\n- End Users: Utilize the system within various applications, benefiting from finely controlled access.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Role and Ability Management: \n   - Support both direct and role-based permissions.\n   - Allow administrators to assign roles and abilities dynamically to users.\n   - Provide a database-driven approach for managing roles and abilities.\n\n2. Permission Caching: \n   - Efficiently cache permissions to enhance system performance.\n\n3. Laravel Integration: \n   - Seamlessly integrate with Laravel’s existing authorization system for consistent application security.\n\n4. Multi-Tenancy Support: \n   - Handle permissions across different application contexts, supporting multi-tenancy.\n\n5. Activity Logging: \n   - Log activities related to assignment and changes in roles and abilities for audit purposes.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Performance:\n   - Ensure adequate performance to handle a large number of permission checks efficiently.\n\n2. Scalability:\n   - Design the system to support scalability as the number of users and roles grow.\n\n3. Security:\n   - Implement robust security measures to prevent unauthorized access and permission escalation.\n\n4. Maintainability:\n   - Ensure the system is easy to maintain and extend with clear documentation and modular design.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0152.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0152\nCREATE TABLE `abilities` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `only_owned` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `options` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `model_has_abilities` (\n  `ability_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `model_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `model_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ability_id`, `model_type`, `model_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `model_has_abilities_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`ability_id`) REFERENCES `abilities` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `level` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tenants` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assigned_roles` (\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `restricted_to_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `restricted_to_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`role_id`, `entity_id`, `entity_type`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assigned_roles_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permissions` (\n  `ability_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `forbidden` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ability_id`, `entity_id`, `entity_type`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permissions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`ability_id`) REFERENCES `abilities` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `activity_logs` (\n  `loggable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `loggable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `action` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`loggable_type`, `loggable_id`, `action`, `user_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `model_has_roles` (\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `model_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `model_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`role_id`, `model_type`, `model_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `model_has_roles_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
